<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Ubykhia</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The land of the Ubykhs around Sochi, a small Black Sea people who led the last Circassian resistance and whose language died out in 1992.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Ubykhs lived on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast and mountain valleys around modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sochi">Sochi</a>, between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/shapsugs">Shapsugs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abkhaz-people">Abkhaz</a>. They spoke a distinct Northwest Caucasian language with a famously large number of consonants. They are recorded as a separate people by the 17th century; earlier history is little known.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ubykh society was led by noble families, above all the Berzeg, together with free communities, and lived by farming, herding and sea trade with Ottoman ports. In 1861 the Ubykhs hosted the Circassian assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sochi">Sochi</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haji-kerantukh-berzeg">Haji Kerantukh Berzeg</a>, which appealed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> for help against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> overran Ubykhia in 1864, and the entire Ubykh people were expelled to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Their language faded in exile; its last fluent speaker, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tevfik-esenc">Tevfik Esenc</a>, died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> in 1992.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 1500 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Caucasus</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:7.24%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1864 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Haji Kerantukh Berzeg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ubykh</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ubykhs, Ubykh</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=ubykhia" aria-label="Ubykhia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5621 184 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ubykhia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6487 457h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5941 440h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5941 440h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=1682&amp;focus=ubykhia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

